A||||12||David||Guard Rail 1, Fiero 0||||1
Z||000000||David||10-23-1999||09:05 AM||||Last Sunday my daughter took my Fiero to work.  On the way home it was raining and she looped the car on an exit ramp of the freeway.  The left rear corner of the car hit the guard rail with such a force that the driver's door will no longer open and the back of the car has been pushed about 1 ½ inches (40mm) to the right in relation to the trunk lid.  The LR rim is broken and that corner's suspension sits at a very bad angle.  The left inner wheel panel is bent into the transmission shift levers, jamming the transmission in gear.<P>My daughter was slightly bruised and was sore for a couple of days, but otherwise OK.  The car though was totaled.  So I am now without my Fiero.  I liked that car, but will probably not get another one.  The thing that I liked best about it was also the thing that caused me the most problems: two seats.  With my family there has bee too many times that I needed to transport three or more, and the van was gone.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||2M4 Dale||10-23-1999||09:54 AM||||Sorry to hear about your Fiero. What model was it? Are you going to sell whats left? I'm looking for parts cars. Hope your daughter is okay. Something about Fieros and women?||||reg||1
Z||000002||David||10-23-1999||10:46 AM||||Dale<P>It's an '86 4 cyl 5 speed that I rebuilt the engine about 30k miles ago.  The car is now in the police inpound lot here in Columbus, OH.  I will let anyone have it for the inpound fee, but the car will have to be towed out.  I have paid $105 in fees just to look at the car.<p>[This message has been edited by David (edited 10-23-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||10-23-1999||11:26 AM||||I can tell you from personal experience that the Columbus, OH police impound lot is one of the biggest scams going in the Buckeye State.  Those freakin' crooks.  I'm really sorry about your Fiero, David.  Sadly, it sounds like your frame is bent, but I'm sure the engine and tranny and lots of other parts are still good...||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||10-23-1999||02:22 PM||||All of the police impounds are scams. That's why you should have a friendly towing service picked out BEFORE an accident. Years ago, a lady ran a stop sign in front of me and I totaled my pickup. I told the cop I wanted him to call my buddy who owned a tow truck. He told me that a tow truck had already been called.<P>I knew that meant, the police impound contract towing service and that the truck would end up in an impound lot where I would be unable to remove any of my personal effects or aftermarket equipment from the truck before the insurance company got their hooks into it.<P>I had to argue with the cop and insist that I had a right to have the towing service of my choice come pick up my truck. I reminded him that I had not committed a crime and if I had to, I wanted to speak to his shift Sgt. directly. He acted all pissed off, but finally relented. Most people are so shaken up and confused after an accident that they don't think about this.<P>My tow bill was about 1/2 of what the police contract towing would of been, plus I had no impound fees or charges to pay. Plus I was able to remove my KC Daylighters, switch out my good 4X4 tires and wheels, pull my stereo and put the factory radio back in, etc. before the insurance company hauled it away. You know they only will give you book value for the vehicle regardless of how many extras you customized your vehicle with.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||10-23-1999||02:37 PM||||<BR>Wow, this is all valuable information.<P>batboy, when you had your truck towed, were you able then to have your truck simply towed to your yard and not to some compound?||||reg||5
Z||000006||theogre||10-23-1999||04:13 PM||||To bad I can't get a look at it...... I'll bet allot of the damage is in the cradle. Might be more repairable than you think.||||reg||1
Z||000007||David||10-23-1999||05:07 PM||||Orge<BR>Anything can be repaired if a person is willing to spend the time and money.  My car is what I consider outside of what I am willing to invest.  It was OK, but not great to start with, and with a whole lot of money I will just be able to make it OK again.  The Fiero was fun, but I'm not in love with it.  The body panels are also cracked behind the sail panels.  I just remenbered that the water overflow tank is pushed into the radiator.  My daughter tells me that she wasn't going too fast.  This is the third car that she has broke since she started driving about two years ago.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||10-23-1999||06:42 PM||||David, I'm glad your daughter came out ok - when it comes down to it, that's really all that matters...<P>Fortunately the Fiero is a very safe car and probably saved your daughter from getting injured more seriously. The impact must have been quite big to create that kind of damage. Most of today's cars have all kinds of safety devices that the Fiero lacks (like airbags), but there are few cars that protects its passengers from side-impacts like the Fiero does.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||David||10-23-1999||07:07 PM||||Cliff<P>I agree with you on both counts.  Cars can be replaced, but people can't.  I've always felt that the Fiero was a safe car to be in.||||reg||1
